'''''Hoya pubicalyx''''' is a species of flowering plant in the genus ''[[Hoya (plant)|Hoya]]'' native to the [[Philippines]].<ref>{{citation |url=https://wcsp.science.kew.org/namedetail.do?name_id=507097 |work=World Checklist of Selected Plant Families |title=Hoya pubicalyx Merr., Philipp. J. Sci., C 13: 331 (1918). }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Frowine |first=Steven A. |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Houseplants_Succulents_For_Dummies/uxfwEAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22Hoya+pubicalyx%22&pg=PA279&printsec=frontcover |title=Houseplants & Succulents For Dummies |date=2024-01-23 |publisher=John Wiley & Sons |isbn=978-1-394-15953-6 |language=en}}</ref> 

== Description ==
Sometimes confused for ''[[Hoya carnosa]]'',<ref>{{Cite book|last=Dortort|first=Fred|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5sJAPAfPkXQC|title=The Timber Press Guide to Succulent Plants of the World: A Comprehensive Reference to More than 2000 Species|date=2014-11-19|publisher=Timber Press|isbn=978-1-60469-357-7|pages=196|language=en}}</ref> it has succulent, [[lanceolate]] foliage and grows in a vining habit epiphytically.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Wanntorp|first=Livia|date=2007|title=Pollinaria of Hoya (Marsdenieae, Apocynaceae): Shedding Light on Molecular Phylogenetics|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25065801|journal=Taxon|volume=56|issue=2|pages=465–478|doi=10.1002/tax.562017|jstor=25065801|issn=0040-0262|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Kept as a houseplant in temperate climates, it can be found in variegated forms in nurseries. The [[Philippine five-centavo coin]] of the [[New Generation Currency Series]] features the flowers of this plant.{{Citation needed|date=April 2025}}

